---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import DocsFireLab from '../../components/DocsFireLab.astro';
import { codeTheme, standaloneCode } from '../../content/examples';
import Documentation from '../../layouts/Documentation.astro';
---
<Documentation>
  <p>This guide uses Fire to demonstrate the shared attachment lifecycle. You can use any <a href="/docs/api#available-effects">available effect</a> with the same time, intensity and cleanup controls. Start with the <a href="/docs/installation">preview app</a>. In <code>main.ts</code>, find your target and its positioned host after mount:</p>
  <div class="docs-code-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Find the target and host"><Code code={`const host = document.querySelector('#host');
const target = document.querySelector('#target');
if (!(host instanceof HTMLElement) || !target) {
  throw new Error('Missing host or target');
}`} lang="ts" theme={codeTheme} /></div>
  <h2 id="attach-fire">Attach Fire</h2>
  <p>Add the following code to the same module. Automatic animation advances effect time. Fonts are ready before capture, and teardown is registered before initialization settles.</p>
  <DocsFireLab title="Tune the attachment" description="Intensity is shared by all effects. Flame height is specific to Fire and recreates this example when changed. The original text remains selectable." />
  <div class="docs-code-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Standalone automatic animation example"><Code code={standaloneCode} lang="ts" theme={codeTheme} /></div>
  <p>On component unmount, call <code>fire.dispose()</code> and remove your own listeners. The pagehide handler covers leaving the page; it does not replace framework teardown.</p>
  <h2 id="use-another-effect">Use another effect</h2>
  <p>Replace <code>attachFire</code> with an attachment such as <code>attachFrost</code> or <code>attachDither</code> and remove <code>flameHeight</code>. The remaining options and handle methods are shared. Intensity changes each effect's appearance; it is not a universal height or opacity setting.</p>
  <p>Dispose the existing attachment before attaching another effect to the same target. The <a href="/effects/">gallery</a> previews every effect; this guide keeps one Fire playground.</p>
  <h2 id="control-time">Control time and intensity</h2>
  <p><code>fire.pause()</code> pauses automatic animation. <code>fire.resume()</code> continues without catching up time spent paused or hidden. Set <code>fire.intensity</code> to a value between 0 and 1. It must be finite and representable as float32; accepted out-of-range values clamp.</p>
  <p>For explicit seeking, choose <code>clock: 'manual'</code> at creation and write <code>fire.time</code> in absolute seconds. Forward and backward seeks reuse the capture. With external control, <code>paused</code> stays true even while time changes; <code>resume()</code> does not advance it.</p>
  <h2 id="refresh-and-cleanup">Refresh and cleanup</h2>
  <p>After changing captured paint, await <code>fire.refresh()</code>. It hides stale output and resolves once this or a newer requested capture is accepted. Observed target mutations and resize can refresh automatically; unobserved stylesheet, hover or ancestor paint changes still need explicit refresh.</p>
  <p>Disposal is safe to call more than once and safe before <code>ready</code>. Removing an observed target or host permanently disposes the effect; create a new attachment after remounting.</p>
  <h2 id="unavailable-output">Unavailable output</h2>
  <p><code>ready</code> resolves after an attempt, including environmental unavailability. Inspect <code>fire.lifecycle</code>, <code>fire.raster</code> and <code>fire.reason</code>. Keep the original content available. After correcting the condition, explicitly await <code>fire.retry()</code> and inspect the result again.</p>
  <p>The default <code>motion: 'respect'</code> suppresses output under reduced motion. Changing the preference back requires an explicit retry. <code>motion: 'static'</code> opts into a still decoration at time zero; it never overrides reduced motion with animation.</p>
  <p>See <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">troubleshooting</a> for reason codes and recovery.</p>
</Documentation>
